<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nur Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amanus Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nur Mountains, the ancient Amanus, are a range in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> running north-east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast near İskenderun and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Their passes, the Syrian and Amanian Gates, carried the main routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the kingdom of Sam'al lay in the valley to their east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nur-mountains.jpg" alt="Nur Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radyokid at Turkish Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Nur_Mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
